        // Domain: Application Lifecycle Management (8%) — 10 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-alm",
            title: "Application Lifecycle Management (8%)",
            color: Color(red: 0.24, green: 0.35, blue: 0.65),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.92, green: 0.93, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "arrow.triangle.2.circlepath",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "alm_obj_1",
                    title: "Assess development methodologies and recommend governance strategies based on customer maturity",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_1", number: 1, question: "Assessing ALM maturity of a customer organization", answer: "ALM maturity signals: Low (ad hoc) = manual deployments, no source control, undocumented processes, tribal knowledge. Medium = repeatable but inconsistent processes, some version control, informal testing. High = automated CI/CD, enforced governance, full traceability. Gaps at each level imply specific risks: low maturity risks data loss and untracked changes; medium risks inconsistency; high maturity requires continuous optimization. Always assess people (skills, roles), process (standards, approvals), and technology (tooling) dimensions together."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_2", number: 2, question: "Ad hoc and repeatable ALM maturity levels", answer: "Ad hoc: no documented processes, each deployment is unique, relies on individual heroics. Risks: high error rate, no rollback. To advance: introduce source control (Git), define a basic deployment runbook, assign a release manager. Repeatable: processes exist but are not enforced consistently. Some version control in use. To advance: formalize branching strategy, add CI validation, document all deployment steps, create sandbox hierarchy."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_3", number: 3, question: "Defined and managed ALM maturity levels", answer: "Defined: standardized processes documented and enforced across all teams; CI/CD pipeline in place; sandbox strategy defined; code reviews required. To advance: add metrics and KPIs (deployment frequency, lead time, MTTR). Managed: processes are measured and controlled; dashboards track release health; governance boards review changes. To advance: automate quality gates, adopt predictive analytics for risk, implement feature flags for safer releases."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_4", number: 4, question: "Optimizing ALM maturity level", answer: "At the optimizing level, the org uses data-driven continuous improvement: post-release retrospectives feed back into process changes, automated test suites are tuned based on defect trends, and deployment pipelines are self-healing. Key practices: chaos engineering, value stream mapping, AI-assisted code review, proactive capacity planning, and regular ALM health audits. The goal is reducing lead time and defect escape rate while increasing deployment frequency."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_5", number: 5, question: "Comparing waterfall vs. agile vs. hybrid methodologies in Salesforce ALM", answer: "Waterfall: fixed requirements, sequential phases, best for highly regulated projects with stable scope (e.g., compliance-driven implementations). Risk: late defect discovery. Agile (Scrum/Kanban): iterative sprints, continuous feedback, best for evolving requirements and experienced teams. Risk: scope creep without governance. Hybrid: agile delivery within waterfall phase gates, ideal for large programs with multiple workstreams or mixed team maturity. Recommend based on project risk level, regulatory requirements, team capability, and release cadence constraints."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_6", number: 6, question: "Risk-based trade-offs between agile speed and governance control", answer: "Agile speeds delivery but can conflict with change control requirements in regulated industries (finance, healthcare). Mitigations: add lightweight approval gates at sprint boundaries instead of blocking every commit; use automated testing as the quality gate; maintain a formal CAB for production deployments while allowing agile development in lower environments. The key is preserving auditability (who changed what, when, and why) without slowing iteration speed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_7", number: 7, question: "Governance strategy selection based on team size, project complexity, and regulatory requirements", answer: "Small teams / low complexity: lightweight governance (PR reviews, basic runbook). Medium complexity: add environment strategy, release calendar, and CAB for production. Large/regulated: full ITIL or SAFe governance with formal change records, risk assessments, traceability matrices, and executive sign-off. Regulatory requirements (SOX, HIPAA) demand immutable audit trails, segregation of duties, and documented approval chains. Always match governance overhead to actual risk."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_8", number: 8, question: "Role of a Center of Excellence (CoE) in Salesforce ALM governance", answer: "A CoE provides centralized standards, best practices, and reusable assets for Salesforce delivery across the enterprise. It owns: coding standards, metadata architecture guidelines, approved tooling list, sandbox strategy, training curriculum, and ALM governance policies. Recommend establishing a CoE when: multiple teams are building on Salesforce independently, technical debt is accumulating, or org mergers require standardization. The CoE enables consistency without stifling team autonomy."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_9", number: 9, question: "Release approval gates in a Salesforce deployment pipeline", answer: "Approval gates are checkpoints that must pass before a deployment can proceed to the next environment. Common gates: code review approval (PR merged), CI validation pass (no test failures, static analysis clean), QA sign-off (test cases executed), UAT sign-off (business users approved), CAB approval (production deployment). Gates enforce quality and governance. They should be automated where possible (CI/CD quality gates) and manual only where human judgment is required (UAT, go/no-go)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "alm_1_10", number: 10, question: "Change Advisory Board (CAB)", answer: "A CAB is a governance body that reviews, approves, or rejects production change requests. Composition typically includes: Release Manager (chair), IT Operations, Security, Business Stakeholders, Architects, and representatives from affected teams. Use CAB for: high-risk or high-impact production changes, emergency changes, and changes affecting multiple systems. CAB ensures changes are assessed for risk, dependencies are identified, rollback plans exist, and accountability is established. In agile contexts, lightweight 'mini-CABs' can review changes at sprint boundaries."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

