            icon: "calendar.badge.clock",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "planning_obj_1",
                    title: "Assess ALM maturity and identify people, technology, and processes required",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_1_1", number: 1, question: "Identifying ALM maturity gaps across people, process, and technology", answer: "For a customer with ad-hoc deployments and no source control: People gaps = no dedicated release manager, developers deploying directly to production, no QA role. Process gaps = no deployment runbook, no change approval workflow, no sandbox strategy. Technology gaps = no version control, no CI/CD tooling, change sets only or manual Metadata API calls. Remediation roadmap: Phase 1 - introduce Git and a sandbox hierarchy. Phase 2 - add CI validation and PR reviews. Phase 3 - automate deployments and add governance gates."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_1_2", number: 2, question: "Developer, QA, admin, and architect roles and handoffs in a mature Salesforce ALM team", answer: "Developer: writes Apex/LWC, creates feature branches, writes unit tests, submits PRs. QA Engineer: writes and executes test cases, validates deployments in QA sandbox, signs off before UAT. Admin/Declarative Developer: configures flows/processes, works in sandbox, tracks changes for source control. Architect: defines environment strategy, approves technical designs, resolves cross-team dependencies. Handoffs: Dev > QA (code merged to integration branch triggers QA deployment) > UAT (QA sign-off triggers UAT) > Release Manager (UAT approval triggers production deployment)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_1_3", number: 3, question: "Release manager role and responsibilities", answer: "The Release Manager coordinates all activities required to move code from development to production. Responsibilities: maintain the release calendar, chair CAB meetings, manage sandbox environments, own the deployment runbook, track release readiness (test pass rates, open defects), communicate release status to stakeholders, and ensure rollback plans exist. They are NOT responsible for writing code but must understand technical risks. In smaller orgs, this role may be filled by a Senior Developer or Architect."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_1_4", number: 4, question: "Source control and CI/CD tool evaluation for a Salesforce ALM pipeline", answer: "Source control options: GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure DevOps Repos. Evaluate on: Salesforce CLI integration, branching model support, PR review workflows, and team familiarity. CI/CD platforms: GitHub Actions, Jenkins, Azure DevOps Pipelines, Bitbucket Pipelines, CircleCI. Evaluate on: Salesforce plugin/orb availability, parallel job support, secret management, cost, and existing enterprise toolchain. Key capability: ability to run 'sf project deploy validate' and 'sf apex run test' as pipeline steps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_1_5", number: 5, question: "Static analysis and deployment tooling evaluation", answer: "Static analysis tools: PMD (open-source, Apex rules), Salesforce Code Analyzer (combines PMD, ESLint, RetireJS, Graph Engine for dataflow analysis). Use PMD for fast CI gate on critical violations; Code Analyzer for deeper security and dataflow analysis. Deployment tools: Salesforce CLI (sf deploy) for scripted/automated deployments, Copado/Gearset/Flosum for managed DevOps with GUI and rollback features, ANT Migration Tool (legacy, largely replaced by CLI). Evaluate on: support for destructive changes, delta deployments, test execution options, and rollback capability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_1_6", number: 6, question: "Building a roadmap to improve ALM maturity from manual change sets", answer: "Sequenced roadmap: (1) Introduce Git source control - retrieve existing org metadata, establish baseline. (2) Define sandbox hierarchy (Dev > Integration > UAT > Staging > Prod). (3) Establish branching strategy (feature branches, main branch mirrors production). (4) Replace change sets with Salesforce CLI deployments. (5) Add CI pipeline for automated validation on PR. (6) Integrate static analysis and test execution gates. (7) Add third-party DevOps tooling (Copado/Gearset) for advanced governance. Training must accompany each phase - tools without process adoption fail."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "planning_obj_2",
                    title: "Understand customer environment risks and articulate appropriate mitigation strategies",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_2_1", number: 1, question: "Risks introduced by direct production changes", answer: "Direct production changes (by admins or developers) bypass source control, CI validation, and testing, creating configuration drift where production no longer matches what is tracked in version control. Mitigation steps to restore ALM integrity: (1) Retrieve the change from production using Salesforce CLI. (2) Commit it to the appropriate branch in source control. (3) Back-propagate it to all lower sandboxes to keep environments in sync. (4) Document the change with a justification and approver. (5) Implement org lockdown policies to prevent recurrence."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_2_2", number: 2, question: "Configuration drift and data leakage as environment risks", answer: "Configuration drift: production diverges from source control and lower environments due to direct changes, sandbox refresh timing gaps, or ad hoc hotfixes. Mitigation: automated drift detection tools (Gearset, Copado), regular environment audits, enforce change-only-through-pipeline policies. Data leakage: sensitive production data (PII, PHI) exposed in non-production sandboxes. Mitigation: data masking/anonymization before sandbox creation, Full Copy sandboxes restricted to authorized personnel only, data classification policies enforced at sandbox creation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_2_3", number: 3, question: "Technical debt accumulation risk in long-lived orgs", answer: "Signals of technical debt: high code complexity scores (PMD violations), large numbers of deprecated API calls, unused metadata components, tightly coupled customizations, absence of test data factories, manual processes that should be automated. Governance controls to prevent further drift: mandatory static analysis in CI pipeline, periodic technical debt sprints, architecture review board approval for new patterns, regular code coverage audits, metadata cleanup campaigns using tools like Salesforce Optimizer or Health Check."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_2_4", number: 4, question: "Mitigating risk of conflicting parallel development streams sharing a single sandbox", answer: "When multiple teams share a single integration sandbox, changes collide - metadata overwrites, broken deployments, and unclear ownership. Mitigation: (1) Allocate dedicated developer sandboxes per team. (2) Use feature branches in Git; merge to integration branch only when ready. (3) Establish a daily/weekly integration sandbox refresh schedule. (4) Use CI pipeline to validate all feature branch deployments in isolation before merging. (5) Consider scratch orgs for complete isolation per feature."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_2_5", number: 5, question: "Sandbox refresh lag as an environment risk", answer: "Sandbox refresh lag occurs when a sandbox has not been refreshed recently and has diverged from production metadata and data, causing tests to fail against stale configurations. Mitigation: define a sandbox refresh schedule aligned to release cadences (e.g., refresh UAT after each production release). Automate post-refresh steps (permission set assignments, custom setting values, test data seeding). Use Partial Copy sandboxes for representative data. Track sandbox age and alert when environments exceed the refresh threshold."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "planning_obj_3",
                    title: "Analyze and recommend the appropriate governance framework",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_3_1", number: 1, question: "Selecting a governance framework (ITIL, SAFe, Agile, custom hybrid)", answer: "ITIL: best for IT operations-heavy orgs needing formal change management, incident management, and CMDB. Use when SLA compliance and audit trails are top priority. SAFe: best for large enterprises running Salesforce as part of a broader enterprise portfolio; provides Program Increments (PI) as release cadence anchors. Agile (Scrum/Kanban): best for small-to-medium product teams with frequent releases and low regulatory burden. Custom Hybrid: most common in Salesforce - agile delivery within PI or release train structure, with CAB-controlled production gates. Match framework to organizational culture; forcing ITIL on a startup culture creates resistance."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_3_2", number: 2, question: "Defining approval gates and escalation paths in a governance framework", answer: "For an enterprise with multiple business units: define tiered approval gates. Dev gate: peer code review + CI pass. Integration gate: integration test pass + Tech Lead approval. UAT gate: business stakeholder sign-off per BU. Staging gate: performance test pass + Security review. Production gate: CAB approval + Release Manager go/no-go. Escalation paths: if a critical defect is found post-UAT, escalate to Release Manager who decides defer or emergency fix path. Document all escalation paths in the release governance runbook so there are no ambiguous decisions under pressure."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_3_3", number: 3, question: "Release coordination across vendor partners in multi-vendor implementations", answer: "Multi-vendor Salesforce programs require a vendor-neutral Release Manager (often the customer's own staff) to own the release train. Key practices: shared release calendar accessible to all vendors, joint CAB meetings, clear API versioning contracts between vendor-developed components, integration testing environment jointly owned and refreshed on a defined schedule, vendor change freeze windows enforced equally. Define handoff points clearly in SLAs and contracts - ambiguous handoffs cause delay and blame-shifting."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_3_4", number: 4, question: "Change ownership and IP rights in multi-vendor implementations", answer: "In multi-vendor Salesforce programs, IP ownership of custom metadata, Apex code, and data models must be contractually defined. Key questions: who owns the source code repository - customer or vendor? Who retains rights if a vendor contract is terminated? Managed packages protect ISV IP but limit customer customization. Unlocked packages or unmanaged code deployments leave IP with whoever controls the source repo. Best practice: customer should own the source control repository and CI/CD pipeline, with vendor teams contributing via PRs, never directly to production."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "planning_obj_4",
                    title: "Recommend strategies to mitigate risks from a new Salesforce seasonal release",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_4_1", number: 1, question: "Strategies for assessing impact of a Salesforce seasonal release", answer: "Three-step approach: (1) Analyze release notes - identify 'Enforce by default' and 'Critical Update' items that auto-activate. (2) Sandbox preview testing - deploy to a preview sandbox 4-6 weeks before production upgrade; run full regression test suite including UI automation. (3) Regression test planning - prioritize tests for features touched by the release (Lightning components, API version changes, Apex governor limits). Key risk areas: API version deprecation, LWC rendering changes, new validation behavior, and changed governor limits."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_4_2", number: 2, question: "Salesforce release preview sandbox window and opt-out mechanisms", answer: "Salesforce upgrades sandbox instances to the upcoming release approximately 4-6 weeks before the production upgrade window. Developer, Developer Pro, and Partial Copy sandboxes on preview instances receive the new release first. Full Copy sandboxes typically do NOT receive preview. Orgs can opt out of the preview window for a specific sandbox by contacting Salesforce support before the cutoff date. The opt-out window is limited - typically 2-3 weeks before the sandbox upgrade. Know which sandbox types are on preview instances in your org."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_4_3", number: 3, question: "Using sandbox preview instances to evaluate seasonal release impacts", answer: "Timing: preview sandboxes receive the new release ~4-6 weeks before production. Access: any user with sandbox login credentials can access the preview sandbox. Testing scope: run the full automated regression suite first; then focus manual testing on components the release notes flag as changed. Considerations: the preview sandbox may have slightly different behavior from final production release (release candidates can be updated). Plan a second test cycle in the week before production upgrade if high-risk changes are identified."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_4_4", number: 4, question: "Impact of a Salesforce seasonal release on an active CI/CD pipeline", answer: "Potential breakage points: (1) API version changes - if your CI pipeline deploys using a deprecated API version, deployments may fail. Update sf CLI and API versions in pipeline scripts before the release. (2) Apex test failures - new platform behavior can break existing tests (e.g., stricter validation, changed default field values). Run full test suite in preview sandbox and fix failures before production upgrade. (3) LWC/Aura breaking changes - component rendering or event behavior changes can break UI tests (Selenium, Provar). Update selectors and test scripts as needed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_4_5", number: 5, question: "Critical update opt-in and auto-activation timelines", answer: "Critical Updates (now 'Release Updates' in Salesforce) are platform behavior changes that require customer opt-in initially but auto-activate after a defined date (typically 1-2 releases later). Architectural implications: deferring auto-activation buys time but creates a deadline you cannot miss. If deferred updates affect your Apex or flows, technical debt accumulates. Best practice: opt in to critical updates in a sandbox as soon as they are available, test proactively, and resolve issues before auto-activation. Surprises at auto-activation = production incidents."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "plan_4_6", number: 6, question: "Communicating seasonal release changes to business stakeholders", answer: "Create a release impact summary document (non-technical) for business stakeholders: what is changing, when, what users will see differently, and what actions stakeholders need to take (e.g., re-approve UAT test cases, attend a demo of new feature behavior). Schedule UAT cycles BEFORE the production upgrade window, not after. Coordinate with Salesforce's published release schedule (typically available 8+ weeks in advance). Set expectations that some post-release support may be needed even with thorough testing."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Domain: System Design (15%) — 20 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-system_design",
