            icon: "square.stack.3d.up.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "sd_obj_1",
                    title: "Explain the advantages of using agile tools to support an agile development process",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_1_1", number: 1, question: "Capabilities and benefits of agile project management tools (Jira, Azure Boards)", answer: "Agile tools provide: backlog management (user stories, epics, priority ordering), sprint planning (capacity planning, sprint boards), velocity tracking, burndown charts, and release planning views. In Salesforce context: user stories map to deployable units of work; sprints align to sandbox deployment cycles; release plans tie to production deployment windows. Benefits: visibility into work in progress, impediment tracking, cross-team coordination, and stakeholder reporting without status meetings. Choose tools the team already uses over adopting something new just for Salesforce."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_1_2", number: 2, question: "Integrating agile tracking tools with Salesforce source control and CI pipelines", answer: "Traceability from story to deployment artifact: (1) Link Git commits to Jira/Azure Boards tickets using smart commit syntax (e.g., 'PROJ-123 Fixed account trigger'). (2) Automate PR creation from branch naming conventions that include ticket IDs. (3) CI pipeline posts build results back to the ticket (GitHub Actions + Jira integration, Azure DevOps native linking). (4) Deployment tools (Copado, DevOps Center) link user stories to specific metadata components. Full traceability enables audit reports: for any production change, trace back to the story, the developer, the PR reviewer, the test results, and the approver."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "sd_obj_2",
                    title: "Evaluate business, technical, and architectural considerations for org strategy",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_2_1", number: 1, question: "Single-org vs. multi-org architecture trade-offs", answer: "Single-org: simpler data model, no integration overhead, shared governor limits, easier cross-BU reporting. Best when BUs share data and processes. Multi-org: full data isolation, independent release cycles, separate governor limit pools, but requires integration layer (middleware, APIs) for cross-org data, duplicated customizations, and more complex governance. Use multi-org when: regulatory/compliance requires data separation, BUs have completely different processes, a subsidiary has its own Salesforce instance post-acquisition, or governor limits are a constraint. Multi-org dramatically increases operational complexity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_2_2", number: 2, question: "Org strategy considerations for an acquisition scenario", answer: "Key decision factors: (1) Data overlap - does the acquired company have duplicate Account/Contact records? Merging requires data migration and deduplication. (2) Process compatibility - if both orgs use different sales processes, merging forces standardization (political risk). (3) Technical complexity - the acquired org may have managed packages or custom metadata that conflicts. (4) Timeline - merging orgs is a 6-18 month project; maintaining separate orgs is faster short-term but costly long-term. (5) Licensing - consolidating may save licenses. Recommendation: evaluate 'merge' vs 'coexist' using a formal feasibility study before committing."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_2_3", number: 3, question: "Governor limits and platform capacity constraints as architectural drivers", answer: "Key Salesforce governor limits that influence org design: SOQL queries (100 synchronous per transaction), DML statements (150), heap size (6MB sync), CPU time (10s sync), async Apex queue limits, API call limits per 24 hours. When a single org approaches these limits due to scale or integration volume, consider: multi-org split (separate high-volume processes to a dedicated org), Platform Events / Async processing to flatten peaks, Bulk API for large data operations. Governor limits should be analyzed during architecture review before design decisions are finalized, not after."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "sd_obj_3",
                    title: "Define an environment (sandbox) strategy",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_3_1", number: 1, question: "Developer and Developer Pro sandbox types", answer: "Developer sandbox: 200MB data storage, 200MB file storage, can be refreshed every 1 day. No production data copied. Best for individual developer feature work and unit testing. Developer Pro: 1GB data storage, 1GB file storage, refresh every 1 day. Supports moderate data volume testing. Best for feature development that requires more metadata or data than a Developer sandbox can handle. Neither type copies production data on creation - data must be manually seeded."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_3_2", number: 2, question: "Partial Copy and Full Copy sandbox types", answer: "Partial Copy: copies all metadata + a sample of production data (up to 10,000 records per object, configurable via sandbox template). Refresh every 5 days. Best for QA/UAT where representative data is needed without full production volume. Full Copy: exact replica of production (all metadata + all data). Refresh every 29 days. Highest cost. Best for performance testing, staging (pre-prod gate), and compliance testing. Full Copy requires data masking before granting access to non-privileged users. Refresh cycle length limits how frequently Full Copy can be used in fast-moving release cycles."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_3_3", number: 3, question: "Sandbox refresh strategy", answer: "When to refresh: (1) After each production release to resync the environment. (2) Before a major test cycle to ensure stable baseline. (3) After a Salesforce seasonal release upgrades production. Impact on active development: refreshing a sandbox destroys all work in progress in that sandbox - always align refreshes with the end of a sprint or release cycle. Sequencing: refresh lower environments (Developer) most frequently; refresh UAT/Staging less often but more deliberately. Communicate planned refreshes via the team calendar so developers can commit and retrieve work before the refresh."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_3_4", number: 4, question: "Designing a sandbox environment hierarchy for multiple parallel streams", answer: "Recommended hierarchy for parallel development: (1) Individual Developer Sandboxes (one per developer/team) for feature development. (2) Integration Sandbox: receives merged feature branches daily for integration testing. (3) QA Sandbox (Partial Copy): receives validated builds for QA test execution. (4) UAT Sandbox (Partial Copy): business-facing environment for user acceptance testing. (5) Staging Sandbox (Full Copy): production-equivalent for final validation, performance testing. (6) Production. Training environment: separate Developer Pro or Partial Copy sandbox, refreshed from a stable release, never connected to the CI pipeline."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_3_5", number: 5, question: "Hotfix environment strategy", answer: "A hotfix sandbox is a dedicated Developer or Developer Pro sandbox kept at (or close to) production parity, used exclusively for emergency production fixes. It is NOT used for ongoing development. Process: (1) Create hotfix branch from the production tag in Git. (2) Develop and test the fix in the hotfix sandbox. (3) Deploy fix to production directly from hotfix branch (bypassing long integration queue). (4) Merge hotfix back into the main development branch immediately. (5) Deploy hotfix branch changes to integration and lower sandboxes to prevent regression. Key rule: hotfix changes MUST be merged back to main branch before the next regular release."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_3_6", number: 6, question: "Scratch org usage in package-based development environment strategy", answer: "Scratch orgs are temporary, fully configurable Salesforce environments created from a definition file. They expire after 1-30 days (default 7). Use scratch orgs to: provide isolated, reproducible environments for each feature (no shared state), test package installation and configuration, run CI builds in clean environments. They replace developer sandboxes in a package-based model. Complement traditional sandboxes: use scratch orgs for unit/component development; use a Partial Copy sandbox for integration/UAT where production-like data is needed. Scratch orgs require a Dev Hub org to be enabled."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "sd_obj_4",
                    title: "Compare, contrast, and recommend components and tools of a successful deployment strategy",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_1", number: 1, question: "Native deployment tools: Change Sets, Salesforce CLI, and ANT Migration Tool", answer: "Change Sets: GUI-based, no version control, point-to-point (only between connected orgs), no destructive changes, good for simple org-to-org migrations. Best for low-maturity teams or infrequent deployments. Salesforce CLI (sf deploy): scriptable, supports all metadata types, integrates with CI pipelines, supports destructive changes, delta deployments. The modern standard. ANT Migration Tool: XML-based, command-line, legacy tool pre-dating SF CLI. No longer recommended; use CLI instead. SF CLI is the right choice for any team investing in automation."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_2", number: 2, question: "Change Set limitations", answer: "Key Change Set limitations: (1) No destructive changes - cannot delete metadata in the same deployment; must delete separately in target org. (2) No version tracking - no history of what was deployed or when. (3) Point-to-point topology only - can only deploy between orgs with a defined deployment connection (cannot deploy to arbitrary targets). (4) Manual dependency ordering - must manually add all dependent components; no automatic resolution. (5) No scripted automation - entirely GUI-driven, cannot integrate with CI/CD. These limitations make Change Sets unsuitable for teams with multiple environments or frequent releases."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_3", number: 3, question: "When Change Set limitations warrant migration to CLI or CI/CD tooling", answer: "Migrate away from Change Sets when: (1) Deployments require destructive changes (field/class deletions). (2) Team has more than 2-3 developers working in parallel. (3) Release frequency is more than once per month. (4) An audit trail of deployments is required. (5) The team wants to introduce automated testing as a gate. (6) Multiple sandboxes need to be kept in sync. (7) A branching strategy has been adopted. Transition path: start by using SF CLI with manual commands, then automate those commands in a CI pipeline."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_4", number: 4, question: "Deployment validation vs. quick deploy", answer: "Deployment validation: runs all specified tests and checks for deployment errors WITHOUT committing changes to the org. Creates a 'validated deployment' record. Quick Deploy: after a successful validation, you can deploy the same set of changes WITHOUT re-running tests within a 10-day window, significantly reducing deployment time. Conditions for quick deploy: validation must have passed with no errors, all required tests passed with 75%+ coverage, no changes to the validated metadata since validation. Risk: if the target org has changed between validation and quick deploy, the deployment may fail or behave unexpectedly. Use in production deployment windows to minimize downtime."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_5", number: 5, question: "CI/CD pipeline architecture for Salesforce", answer: "Stages: (1) Build: resolve metadata delta (what changed since last deployment), package components for deployment. (2) Validate: run 'sf project deploy validate' against target org - checks metadata validity and runs tests without committing. (3) Test: run Apex test suite (RunSpecifiedTests or RunLocalTests), generate coverage report, run static analysis (PMD/Code Analyzer), optionally run UI automation (Provar/CRT). (4) Deploy: if all gates pass, run 'sf project deploy start' to commit changes to the target org. (5) Post-deploy: run post-deployment scripts (assign permission sets, activate flows, seed data). Tools: GitHub Actions/Jenkins orchestrate; SF CLI executes; Slack/Teams notify."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_6", number: 6, question: "Salesforce DevOps Center", answer: "DevOps Center is Salesforce's native release management layer built on top of the platform itself. It integrates with GitHub for source control and provides a GUI for managing work items (user stories), pipelines, and deployments without needing third-party tools. Key capabilities: pipeline visualization, work item tracking, change bundling, and GitHub-based source truth. Best for: teams moving off Change Sets who want a guided, native experience without third-party tooling cost. Preferred over third-party tools when: budget constraints exist, team is not technically ready for raw CI/CD pipeline management, and GitHub is already the SCM of choice."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_7", number: 7, question: "Third-party deployment tools (Copado, Gearset, Flosum)", answer: "Copado: enterprise-grade Salesforce DevOps platform with full pipeline management, user story tracking, conflict resolution, rollback, compliance features, and AI-assisted capabilities (Agentia). Best for large enterprises needing full ALM governance. Gearset: developer-friendly, strong UI for comparing orgs and deploying delta changes, CI/CD integration, backup/restore. Best for teams wanting quick adoption with minimal setup. Flosum: native Salesforce AppExchange app, all data stays in Salesforce. Best for orgs with strict data sovereignty requirements. All three reduce CLI complexity but add licensing cost and vendor dependency."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_8", number: 8, question: "CI/CD platform selection for Salesforce", answer: "GitHub Actions: native to GitHub repos, excellent Salesforce community support (sfdx-cli action, salesforce-cca action), YAML-based, free tier available. Best for teams already on GitHub. Jenkins: highly customizable, self-hosted, large plugin ecosystem, SF CLI Jenkinsfile patterns well-documented. Best for enterprises with existing Jenkins infrastructure and need for complex pipelines. Azure DevOps Pipelines: integrated with Azure Boards and Azure Repos, YAML or classic editor, good for Microsoft-stack enterprises. Bitbucket Pipelines: tightly integrated with Bitbucket repos, YAML-based, simpler than Jenkins. Evaluate based on: existing toolchain, team skills, hosting requirements, and pipeline complexity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "sd_4_9", number: 9, question: "Metadata API deployment order dependencies", answer: "Salesforce Metadata API requires components to be deployed in dependency order or in the same deployment package. Key dependency rules: (1) Custom Objects must deploy before their fields. (2) Custom Fields must deploy before Page Layouts and Compact Layouts that reference them. (3) Apex Classes must deploy before Apex Triggers that reference them. (4) Permission Sets must deploy before assigning them in post-deploy scripts. (5) Flows must deploy before Process Builders that call them. Failure to respect dependency order causes deployment errors. CI tools like Copado handle dependency ordering automatically; raw CLI deployments require you to manage this manually or deploy all components in a single package."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Domain: Building (14%) — 24 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-building",
            title: "Building (14%)",
            color: Color(red: 0.60, green: 0.35, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.98, green: 0.93, blue: 0.90),
            icon: "hammer.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "build_obj_1",
                    title: "Explain and recommend source control branching, versioning, and merging strategies",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_1_1", number: 1, question: "Git branching strategies for Salesforce development", answer: "Trunk-based development: all developers commit frequently (daily) to a single main branch; feature flags used to hide incomplete work; lowest merge conflict risk; requires high test automation maturity. Best for high-maturity teams with strong CI. Gitflow: long-lived develop, release, and hotfix branches; releases are bundled and merged to main; high governance but high merge conflict risk in large metadata files. Best for scheduled release cadences. Feature branching: one branch per user story/feature, merged to main via PR; balances isolation and integration speed. Most common in Salesforce. Choose based on team size, release frequency, and test automation maturity."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_1_2", number: 2, question: "Branching strategy for a customer with a hotfix alongside an active feature branch", answer: "Recommended approach: (1) Create a hotfix branch from the production release tag (not from main/develop, which may contain unreleased features). (2) Develop and test the fix in the hotfix branch. (3) Merge the hotfix directly to production (or the production branch). (4) Immediately merge the hotfix branch back into the main/develop branch and all active feature branches to prevent regression. (5) Tag the hotfix release in Git. Risk: if feature branches are not updated with the hotfix, the next regular release will revert the fix. Use merge or rebase strategy consistently."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_1_3", number: 3, question: "Handling declarative changes alongside programmatic changes in source control", answer: "Declarative changes (flows, layouts, approval processes, custom fields) are stored as XML metadata files in source control alongside Apex code. Use 'sf project retrieve start' to pull declarative changes from a sandbox into the local source format. Track flows as FlowDefinition and Flow metadata types. Layouts as Layout type. Key challenge: admins making declarative changes in a sandbox must remember to retrieve and commit those changes, or they will be lost on sandbox refresh. Solution: establish a 'retrieve and commit' workflow step as part of every admin's daily process. Consider using source-tracked orgs (scratch orgs or opted-in sandboxes) to automate change detection."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_1_4", number: 4, question: "Managing merge conflicts in Salesforce XML metadata", answer: "Salesforce metadata files (especially Profiles, Permission Sets, and Object XML) are large, auto-generated XML files that conflict frequently when multiple developers modify the same file. Prevention strategies: (1) Use Permission Sets instead of Profiles to reduce Profile file size and conflict surface. (2) Split object metadata into sub-files (source format does this automatically). (3) Keep feature branches short-lived to reduce divergence. Resolution strategies: use a git merge tool with XML awareness; resolve conflicts by keeping both sets of changes and removing only the XML diff markers. Tooling: Gearset and Copado have built-in conflict resolution UIs that compare metadata semantically rather than as raw XML."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_1_5", number: 5, question: "Semantic versioning and tagging strategies in Salesforce source control", answer: "Apply Git tags at every production deployment using semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH). MAJOR: breaking changes or significant feature releases. MINOR: new features, backward-compatible. PATCH: bug fixes / hotfixes. Tag format example: 'v2.3.1'. Benefits: (1) Clear rollback point - if a production issue occurs, revert to the previous tag. (2) Audit trail - associate a Git tag with a deployment record and CAB approval. (3) Package versioning alignment - unlocked package versions can map to Git tags. Script tagging into the CI/CD pipeline post-successful production deployment so tagging is never forgotten."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "build_obj_2",
                    title: "Describe appropriate approaches to test data strategy and unit tests",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_1", number: 1, question: "Minimum Apex code coverage requirements for production deployment", answer: "Salesforce requires at minimum 75% org-wide Apex code coverage to deploy to production. Additionally, every Apex trigger must have at least 1% coverage. What counts: executable lines covered by at least one test method. What does NOT count: comments, blank lines, class/method declarations, System.debug statements, test class code itself. Coverage is calculated org-wide (all Apex in the org), not per-class. Deploying a new class with 0% coverage is allowed as long as org-wide total remains at or above 75%. Avoid 'coverage-only' tests that hit lines without asserting behavior."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_2", number: 2, question: "Positive path and negative path Apex unit test design", answer: "Positive path tests verify that the code works correctly under expected conditions: valid input, correct record creation, expected field values updated, expected trigger behavior. Negative path tests verify that the code fails gracefully under invalid conditions: null inputs, missing required fields, unauthorized access, bulk limits exceeded, duplicate records. Both are required for robust test coverage. Negative path tests often catch the real bugs. Example: a positive test creates a valid Opportunity and confirms the stage updates correctly; a negative test attempts to create an Opportunity with a null Account and confirms a meaningful exception or error message is returned."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_3", number: 3, question: "Permission-based unit tests using System.runAs()", answer: "System.runAs(user) executes a block of test code in the context of a specified user, enforcing sharing rules, FLS, and CRUD permissions. Use it to: (1) Test that users without a certain permission set cannot access or modify records. (2) Verify record visibility under org-wide defaults and sharing rules. (3) Confirm that FLS is enforced for sensitive fields. Pattern: create a test user with a specific profile/permission set, call System.runAs(testUser) { ... } around the code under test. Important: System.runAs does NOT enforce governor limits per-user in tests; it only enforces security context. Every test that involves record-level security should use System.runAs."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_4", number: 4, question: "Test data isolation in Apex: @isTest(SeeAllData=false)", answer: "@isTest(SeeAllData=false) is the default behavior: tests cannot see any org data; all data must be created within the test. This is the correct and safe default. SeeAllData=true allows tests to see real org data; this makes tests environment-dependent and fragile (they pass in one sandbox, fail in another because data differs). Use SeeAllData=true only when absolutely required (e.g., testing against standard price books, which require SeeAllData=true). Risks of SeeAllData=true: tests are brittle, data-dependent, hard to maintain, and can cause false positives/negatives depending on org state."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_5", number: 5, question: "Test data factory patterns in Apex", answer: "Test data factory: a shared Apex class (e.g., TestDataFactory) with static methods that create and optionally insert standard test records. Benefits: DRY principle - test data creation logic in one place; consistent test records across all tests; easy to update when schema changes. @testSetup: a method annotated @testSetup runs once before all test methods in the class and creates shared data. More efficient than creating data in each test method. Prefer @testSetup for data shared across many tests in the same class; prefer factory methods for complex, varied, or cross-class data needs. Combine both: factory generates records, @testSetup inserts them once."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_6", number: 6, question: "Apex mock frameworks for callout testing", answer: "Apex tests cannot make real HTTP or web service callouts. Two interfaces to mock them: (1) HttpCalloutMock: implement the respond() method to return a fake HttpResponse for REST/HTTP callouts. Set it with Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, mockInstance). (2) WebServiceMock (StubProvider): implement the doInvoke() method to simulate SOAP web service responses. Set it with Test.setMock(WebServiceMock.class, mockInstance). Pattern: create a mock class that implements the interface, return a predefined response, then assert that your Apex code processes the response correctly. This isolates the unit test from external dependencies and makes it deterministic."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_7", number: 7, question: "Bulk and large data volume Apex unit test design", answer: "Salesforce triggers and batch Apex must handle bulk operations (up to 200 records per trigger invocation in synchronous DML, up to 2000 records per batch chunk). Unit tests must verify bulk behavior: create 200 records in a single DML operation and assert the trigger handles all 200 correctly. Test for: SOQL queries not exceeding limits in bulk context, DML statements not exceeding limits, CPU time remaining within limits, no row-by-row processing. A trigger that works for 1 record but fails at 200 is a critical production bug. Always include at least one bulk test per trigger."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_2_8", number: 8, question: "Large data volume testing in Apex using Test.startTest/stopTest", answer: "Test.startTest() resets governor limit counters for the code between startTest() and Test.stopTest(). This allows tests to set up data before startTest() without consuming the limits the code under test will use. stopTest() also flushes async operations (future methods, queueable, batch Apex scheduled within startTest). To simulate large data volumes: create large record lists in @testSetup (or before startTest), then call startTest(), trigger your Apex, call stopTest(), and assert results. Note: actual data volume is still capped at DML limits in tests - you cannot test true millions-of-records scenarios in unit tests; use sandbox performance testing for that."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "build_obj_3",
                    title: "Describe the appropriate development model and development environment",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_1", number: 1, question: "Org-based development vs. package-based development", answer: "Org-based development: the production org is the system of record; changes are made in sandboxes and deployed to production; source control may be used but the org is the truth. Simpler, familiar to admins. Package-based development: source control is the system of record; changes are assembled into packages (unlocked or managed) that are installed into orgs; the org is just a package installation target. More scalable, enables modular architecture, better for large teams. Key differences: source tracking (packages track what is installed), deployment mechanism (package installation vs. metadata deploy), and team collaboration model (package subscribers vs. sandbox deployments)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_2", number: 2, question: "Salesforce CLI source-tracking in org-based development", answer: "'sf project deploy start' (formerly sfdx force:source:push) pushes local source changes to a source-tracked org. 'sf project retrieve start' (formerly sfdx force:source:pull) pulls org changes to local source. Source tracking is available for scratch orgs by default and for opted-in sandboxes (source tracking sandboxes, introduced in recent releases). This differs from the traditional Metadata API model where you specify which components to retrieve/deploy explicitly - source tracking automatically detects what changed. Source tracking sandboxes give org-based teams scratch-org-like workflow without full package-based development adoption."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_3", number: 3, question: "Scratch org capabilities and limitations", answer: "Capabilities: fully configurable via scratch org definition JSON file (features, org preferences, edition), can be created in seconds, can be seeded with data via scripts, enable source tracking, isolated per developer/CI run, no licensing cost (included with Dev Hub). Limitations: expire after 1-30 days (default 7), maximum 40 active per Dev Hub (200 with expanded allocation), no production data, features must be enabled in definition file (cannot just test with production config), managed package installations can be slow. Best for: feature development, CI testing, package development. Not ideal for: UAT (needs representative data), performance testing (needs data volume)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_4", number: 4, question: "Choosing between scratch orgs and developer sandboxes", answer: "Choose scratch orgs when: team is doing package-based development, full isolation per feature is needed, CI pipeline needs clean environments per build, team is comfortable with SF CLI and Dev Hub. Choose developer sandboxes when: team is doing org-based development, long-running development tasks span multiple weeks, declarative admins need a persistent environment, team lacks CLI expertise. Cost: scratch orgs are included with Dev Hub license (typically included with Enterprise+); developer sandboxes have a per-sandbox cost but are typically included in most Salesforce editions. Team size matters: scratch orgs scale better for large dev teams needing many isolated environments."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_5", number: 5, question: "Scratch org expiry and lifecycle management", answer: "Default scratch org expiry: 7 days. Maximum: 30 days. When a scratch org expires, all work in it is lost - metadata and data are deleted. Implications for long-running feature branches: if a feature takes longer than the scratch org lifetime, you must either extend the scratch org (up to 30 days max) or recreate and re-push. Strategies to avoid losing work: commit and push to source control daily (the source control branch is the permanent record, not the scratch org). Use 'sf org create scratch --duration-days 30' for long features. Document data seeding scripts so a new scratch org can be re-seeded quickly if needed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_6", number: 6, question: "sfdx-project.json structure and packageDirectories configuration", answer: "The sfdx-project.json file is the project manifest for Salesforce DX projects. Key properties: 'packageDirectories' defines which source folders belong to which package (path, package name, version number, dependencies). 'sourceApiVersion' sets the Metadata API version for source format. 'namespace' sets the namespace prefix (required for managed packages). 'plugins' registers SF CLI plugin settings. In a multi-package project, each package has its own entry in packageDirectories with its own dependencies array. The order of entries determines dependency resolution. Correctly structured sfdx-project.json is required for 'sf package version create' to work."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_3_7", number: 7, question: "Unlocked packages as a migration path from org-based to package-based development", answer: "Unlocked packages are the recommended intermediate step from org-based to fully package-based development. They impose package structure (components must be explicitly included) but do not require a namespace or ISV licensing. Migration path: (1) Identify a bounded domain of metadata (e.g., all Account-related customizations). (2) Extract those components into a package directory. (3) Create the unlocked package and generate a version. (4) Install the package in target orgs instead of deploying raw metadata. Prerequisites: source control with SF DX project format, Dev Hub enabled, team trained on CLI package commands. Start with a small, low-risk domain to build confidence before migrating the full org."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "build_obj_4",
                    title: "Describe methods to ensure delivery of quality code",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_4_1", number: 1, question: "Defining Salesforce Apex and LWC coding standards", answer: "Apex standards: use a trigger handler framework (one trigger per object, handler class with separate methods per event). Bulkify all DML and SOQL (no SOQL/DML in loops). Use 'with sharing' by default (explicit sharing model on every class). Naming conventions: TriggerHandler_Object, Service classes, Selector classes for SOQL. Document public methods with JSDoc-style comments. LWC standards: component per concern, no direct DOM manipulation outside @track properties, use wire for data, no hardcoded IDs. Enforce standards via: code review checklist, PR templates, static analysis rules in CI pipeline."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_4_2", number: 2, question: "Pull request review process for Salesforce development", answer: "A PR review ensures code quality before it reaches the integration branch. Reviewers should check: (1) No SOQL/DML in loops. (2) Proper bulkification. (3) Unit tests with meaningful assertions (not just coverage). (4) Sharing model declared on every class. (5) No hardcoded IDs or org-specific values. (6) Metadata dependencies included (e.g., custom labels, custom settings). (7) Destructive changes documented and intentional. Required approvals: at minimum 1 peer reviewer + tech lead for major changes. PRs gate CI execution: CI pipeline runs only when PR is opened or updated, ensuring validation before merge. Failed CI blocks merge."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_4_3", number: 3, question: "PMD and Salesforce Code Analyzer for Apex static analysis", answer: "PMD: open-source static analysis tool with Apex rule sets. Rules enforced: no SOQL in loops (ApexSOQLInjection, AvoidSoqlInLoops), no DML in loops (AvoidDmlStatementsInLoops), empty catch blocks, unused variables, overly complex methods. Fast to run, integrates with CI. Salesforce Code Analyzer (scanner): built on PMD but adds ESLint (LWC), RetireJS (JS dependencies), and Graph Engine for dataflow analysis (detects SOQL injection, XSS). More comprehensive but slower. Use PMD for fast CI gate on Apex; use Code Analyzer for scheduled deep analysis (weekly security scans). Configure severity thresholds: fail build on critical/high violations, report-only on medium/low."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "build_4_4", number: 4, question: "Integrating static analysis into the CI pipeline", answer: "Two governance modes: (1) Hard gate: build FAILS if static analysis finds violations above a threshold (e.g., any 'Critical' PMD violation blocks merge). This enforces zero tolerance for the worst patterns. (2) Reporting-only gate: violations are reported as warnings in the PR but do not block merge; used during team onboarding or for lower-severity rules. Best practice: start with reporting-only to establish a baseline, then progressively tighten the threshold as the team addresses violations. Avoid adding a hard gate on a codebase with hundreds of existing violations - it will block all work. Use a 'ratchet' approach: fail only on NEW violations introduced in the PR, not pre-existing ones."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Domain: Deploying (14%) — 16 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-deploying",
            title: "Deploying (14%)",
            color: Color(red: 0.15, green: 0.50, blue: 0.55),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.97),
            icon: "shippingbox.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "deploy_obj_1",
                    title: "Describe capabilities, limitations, and considerations of Metadata and Tooling APIs",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_1_1", number: 1, question: "Metadata API capabilities for deployment", answer: "Metadata API supports deploying and retrieving most Salesforce metadata types (custom objects, fields, Apex classes, triggers, flows, layouts, reports, etc.). Deployment is asynchronous: submit a deploy request, poll for status. Supports deployment of up to 10,000 files per deploy. Can be used via SF CLI (which wraps the API), third-party tools, or direct API calls. Supports test execution options (RunLocalTests, RunSpecifiedTests, RunAllTests, NoTestRun) at deploy time. Key capability: can deploy to any connected Salesforce org, not just point-to-point like Change Sets."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_1_2", number: 2, question: "Metadata API limitations: unsupported metadata types", answer: "Not all Salesforce metadata is deployable via Metadata API. Examples of limited or unsupported types: (1) Some standard objects' settings (cannot deploy certain standard field configurations). (2) Reports and dashboards: can deploy report type metadata but not report data/results. (3) Territory management configurations. (4) Salesforce CPQ metadata (some types). (5) Experience Cloud (Community) site content. (6) Certain custom settings values (the type is deployable, but data requires separate approach). Manual deployment candidates: connected app OAuth secrets, named credentials with secrets, certain org-wide email settings, some platform encryption settings."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_1_3", number: 3, question: "Tooling API vs. Metadata API for deployment", answer: "Tooling API is designed for incremental, real-time development operations: compile individual Apex classes synchronously, retrieve code coverage for specific classes, query metadata for IDE tooling. Use Tooling API when: you need synchronous Apex compilation feedback, you are building a developer tooling integration, or you need to query metadata attributes not exposed via Metadata API. Use Metadata API when: you are deploying a set of components as part of a release (full or delta deployments), or running tests as part of a CI validation. SF CLI uses both under the hood: push/retrieve uses Metadata API; language server uses Tooling API."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_1_4", number: 4, question: "Deploying metadata with dependencies", answer: "Cross-component references must be respected in deployments. If Class A references Class B, both must be deployed together (or Class B must already exist in the target org). Common dependency failures: deploying a Flow that references a custom field before the custom field is deployed; deploying an Apex class that references a custom label not included in the package. Solutions: (1) Deploy all components in a single deployment package (safest). (2) Use a two-pass deployment: deploy foundational components first (objects, fields, classes), then deploy the components that depend on them. (3) Use tools like Copado that automatically resolve dependency order."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_1_5", number: 5, question: "Destructive changes in Salesforce deployments", answer: "To delete metadata components via Metadata API, include a destructiveChanges.xml file (post-deploy deletions) or destructiveChangesPre.xml (pre-deploy deletions) in the deployment package alongside a package.xml. destructiveChanges.xml lists components to delete after the additive changes are deployed. destructiveChangesPre.xml deletes components BEFORE additive changes are applied (use when you are replacing a component that conflicts with a new one). Limitation: cannot combine destructive changes and additive changes in a Change Set deployment (CLI does not have this limitation). Risk: once deleted, metadata is gone (no native rollback); always backup before destructive deployments."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_1_6", number: 6, question: "Deployment rollback strategies", answer: "Salesforce has no native one-click rollback for Metadata API deployments. Mitigation patterns: (1) Backup before deployment: retrieve current production metadata and store it as a 'rollback package' in source control. (2) Destructive rollback: if new code/config caused issues, deploy the previous version of the metadata. (3) Version control revert: revert the Git tag to the previous release and redeploy. (4) Feature flags: disable a feature via custom metadata or permission set without redeployment. (5) Quick deploy of previous validation: if you validated the previous version recently, quick deploy it back. Document rollback procedures in the deployment runbook and test them."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "deploy_obj_2",
                    title: "Describe approaches to handle pre and post-deployment steps",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_2_1", number: 1, question: "Deployment runbook design", answer: "A deployment runbook is a step-by-step document that ensures consistent, error-free production deployments. Contents: (1) Pre-deployment checklist (steps to execute before the deployment starts). (2) Deployment steps (commands to run, order, expected outputs). (3) Validation steps (smoke tests to run immediately after deployment). (4) Rollback procedures (what to do if deployment fails or causes issues). (5) Communication plan (who to notify at each step). (6) Go/no-go decision criteria. Runbooks should be version-controlled alongside the deployment package they describe. The Release Manager owns the runbook; the deployment team follows it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_2_2", number: 2, question: "Pre-deployment checklist", answer: "Before starting a production deployment: (1) Communicate maintenance window to users and stakeholders. (2) Disable automation that could interfere: deactivate Flows, Process Builders, or workflow rules that would trigger on test/deployment records. (3) Pause scheduled batch jobs that could run during deployment. (4) Back up current production metadata (retrieve to a 'pre-deploy' branch in source control). (5) Verify validation run passed (or run a fresh validation). (6) Confirm all approval gates are complete (CAB approved, UAT signed off). (7) Ensure rollback package is ready. (8) Notify the support team to be on standby."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_2_3", number: 3, question: "Managing user access before and during a deployment window", answer: "For high-risk deployments, consider: (1) Enabling a maintenance page or message (Salesforce does not have a native maintenance mode; use a custom login flow or outbound messaging). (2) Restricting access to specific profiles by temporarily adding an IP restriction or login hours restriction to force users out. (3) For truly zero-impact deployments: use feature flags (custom metadata or permission sets) to deploy code without exposing it to users, then activate after deployment. (4) After deployment: re-enable login access and notify users. User access management steps should be in the deployment runbook with the specific profiles/permission sets to modify."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_2_4", number: 4, question: "Post-deployment steps", answer: "Immediately after a successful deployment: (1) Re-enable deactivated automations (Flows, Process Builders, workflow rules). (2) Assign new permission sets to the appropriate users or public groups. (3) Activate new record types and update page layout assignments. (4) Run post-deployment data scripts (seed lookup data, update custom setting values, assign roles). (5) Execute smoke tests / validation scripts to confirm key business processes work. (6) Re-schedule batch jobs that were paused. (7) Notify stakeholders that deployment is complete and the system is available. Document all post-deployment steps in the runbook with expected completion time per step."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_2_5", number: 5, question: "Post-deployment data migrations", answer: "Data migrations are required when: (1) A new field is introduced that must be populated for existing records (backfill). (2) Records must be reassigned to new record types. (3) A new object is introduced and existing data must be migrated from another object. (4) Custom setting or custom metadata values must be set in production (cannot deploy via package if values are environment-specific). Tools: Data Loader (manual, GUI), SF CLI data commands (scriptable), Batch Apex (for complex transformations), or ETL tools (MuleSoft, Informatica). Always run data migrations in a staging sandbox first. Have a rollback script for data migrations."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_2_6", number: 6, question: "Reports, dashboards, connected apps, and custom settings: Metadata API limitations and manual candidates", answer: "Reports: Report Type metadata is deployable; actual report definitions are deployable via Metadata API but can be fragile if referenced fields/objects differ. Report subscriptions and scheduled reports are not deployable. Dashboards: similar to reports - dashboard XML is deployable but running user and schedule settings are manual. Connected Apps: the metadata is deployable but OAuth secrets (consumer key/secret) must be reconfigured manually in the target org. Custom Settings: the object type is deployable; the actual data/records within Custom Settings must be deployed separately (via Data Loader, CLI data commands, or included in package as Default values for Hierarchy type). These items should be explicitly listed in the deployment runbook as manual steps."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "deploy_obj_3",
                    title: "Describe approaches to manage and deploy technical reference data",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_3_1", number: 1, question: "Distinguishing technical reference data from transactional data", answer: "Technical reference data: configuration stored as object records that drives application behavior. Examples: picklist value sets (if stored as custom metadata), integration endpoints in Custom Settings, feature flag records in Custom Metadata Types, tax rates in a custom object used by code logic. Transactional data: business records created by users/integrations (Accounts, Opportunities, Cases). Technical reference data must be deployed as part of the release process because code depends on it existing correctly. Transactional data is never deployed between environments (it is seeded for testing, masked for privacy). The boundary: if your code queries it, it is reference data and must be managed as a deployment artifact."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_3_2", number: 2, question: "Custom Metadata Types vs. Custom Settings for storing reference data", answer: "Custom Metadata Types (CMT): records are metadata - they can be included in packages, deployed via Metadata API, and version-controlled. Support SOQL queries in Apex. Deployable across environments as part of a deployment package. Best for configuration that must be consistent across orgs and environment-agnostic or environment-specific via named records. Custom Settings: object data, NOT metadata. Cannot be deployed via standard Metadata API (only the object structure is deployable; the records require Data Loader or a post-deploy script). Support Hierarchy type (override at org, profile, user level). Use CMT when deploying configuration values is part of the release. Use Custom Settings for data that may differ per user/profile."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_3_3", number: 3, question: "Managing environment-specific reference data using Custom Metadata with deployment-time overrides", answer: "For environment-specific configuration (e.g., different integration endpoints per environment): Create CMT records with an environment identifier field (e.g., Environment__c = 'Production', 'Staging', 'Dev'). Apex code queries the correct record at runtime based on a deterministic check. Alternatively, create separate CMT records per environment and deploy only the relevant record to each org. For Custom Settings: use a post-deployment script to set the correct values per environment (automate this in the CI/CD pipeline post-deploy step). Custom Settings with manual updates are error-prone; prefer CMT where possible."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "deploy_3_4", number: 4, question: "Deploying reference data using Salesforce CLI data commands, Data Loader, or package records", answer: "Salesforce CLI data commands ('sf data upsert bulk', 'sf data import tree'): scriptable, integrates with CI/CD pipeline, supports JSON/CSV, good for repeatable seeding of CMT records or Custom Setting values. Best for automating post-deploy data steps. Data Loader: GUI-based, batch upserts/inserts, uses CSV files. Good for one-time or infrequent data migrations. Managed/Unlocked package records: Custom Metadata records can be included directly in a package and installed with it - the cleanest approach for CMT configuration. Unlocked packages can ship CMT records as part of the package version. Trade-off: package records require package version creation for every config change; CLI scripts are faster for frequent config updates."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Domain: Testing (13%) — 15 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-testing",
            title: "Testing (13%)",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.35),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.97, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "testtube.2",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "test_obj_1",
                    title: "Describe and recommend an appropriate testing methodology",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_1_1", number: 1, question: "Testing pyramid for Salesforce", answer: "Unit tests (base of pyramid, most numerous): Apex unit tests, individual class/trigger behavior, fast, isolated, developer-owned. Integration tests (middle): test interactions between Apex components, flows, and external systems; run in a sandbox; QA-owned. System tests: end-to-end business process testing in a production-like environment; test the full stack from UI to database; QA and BA-owned. UAT (top, fewest): business stakeholder validation of acceptance criteria; human-driven; business-owned. The pyramid shape means most tests should be at the unit level (cheap, fast) with progressively fewer as you go up (expensive, slow). Invert the pyramid and you get slow, brittle test suites."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_1_2", number: 2, question: "Shift-left testing strategy for Salesforce", answer: "Shift-left means moving testing earlier in the development lifecycle: developers write unit tests before or alongside code (TDD/BDD), static analysis runs on every commit (not just before release), integration tests run in CI on every PR (not just in UAT). Benefits: defects found earlier cost less to fix (the 'rule of 10x' - a bug found in dev costs 1x; in UAT costs 10x; in production costs 100x). Shift-left in Salesforce: run Apex tests in scratch org during development; use Apex test frameworks (SFDC Unit Test); integrate PMD/Code Analyzer in PR pipeline; require QA sandbox validation before UAT, not after feature code freeze."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_1_3", number: 3, question: "Recommending test methodology for an org with complex automation and integrations", answer: "For complex orgs: (1) Apex unit tests cover all trigger and service logic (bulkified, positive and negative paths). (2) Apex integration tests cover cross-object behavior and flow invocations from Apex. (3) Flow and process builder tests in sandbox (Salesforce does not currently have a robust automated flow test framework beyond Apex invocations). (4) End-to-end tests using Provar or Copado Robotic Testing for critical business processes (Order-to-Cash, Lead-to-Opportunity). (5) Manual exploratory testing for edge cases and new UI features. (6) Integration endpoint mocking for external system calls. Balance automation (reliable, repeatable) with strategic manual testing (adaptive, exploratory)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_1_4", number: 4, question: "Regression test strategy for a high-volume, frequently-released Salesforce org", answer: "Automate: high-value, stable, frequently-run tests (core business process flows, critical Apex logic, standard object interactions). These are the tests that, if they fail, mean a release is not ready. Test manually: new features with unstable UIs, edge cases that are rare but high-impact, exploratory testing of new functionality. Deprioritize: low-risk configuration changes (report layouts, email templates) that do not interact with code. Regression suite management: review and prune the suite quarterly - remove tests for deprecated features, add tests for new high-risk areas. Keep the automated suite under 30 minutes to maintain CI pipeline speed."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_1_5", number: 5, question: "Browser/UI automation testing for Salesforce Lightning", answer: "UI automation tools for Salesforce Lightning: Selenium (open-source, requires significant Salesforce-specific framework work, Lightning-specific selectors are complex), Provar (purpose-built for Salesforce, no-code/low-code test creation, built-in Lightning support), Copado Robotic Testing / Agentia Testing (Robot Framework-based, AI-assisted test creation, integrates with Copado pipelines). When to introduce: after the core Apex unit test layer is stable; when manual regression of critical UI flows takes more than 2-3 hours per release; when release frequency increases to weekly or more. Start with 5-10 high-value end-to-end scenarios rather than trying to automate everything immediately."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "test_obj_2",
                    title: "Describe the appropriate test execution methodology and coverage requirements",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_2_1", number: 1, question: "Code coverage calculation in Salesforce", answer: "Org-wide coverage = (total executable lines covered by at least one test) / (total executable lines in the org). Calculated across ALL Apex in the org, not per-class. A coverage check is triggered: at every production deployment that includes Apex (if RunLocalTests or higher is specified), when you manually run tests. How to interpret: if org-wide is 74%, you cannot deploy any Apex to production. If it is 80%, you have a 5% buffer. Coverage per class: shown in Developer Console and SF CLI test output. Classes with 0% coverage are the biggest risk - one such class added to a high-coverage org can drag the overall percentage below 75%."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_2_2", number: 2, question: "Apex test execution options", answer: "Three main options: RunLocalTests: runs all tests in the org EXCEPT those from managed packages. Most common for production deployments. RunAllTests: runs ALL tests including managed package tests. Use when managed package tests contribute to coverage or when a full regression is required. RunSpecifiedTests: runs only the test classes you specify. Fastest option for CI on a specific feature. Coverage implications: RunSpecifiedTests only counts coverage for the classes touched by those specific tests - org-wide coverage may appear low. For production deployment with RunSpecifiedTests, Salesforce validates that the DEPLOYED components have at least 75% coverage from those specified tests, not org-wide."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_2_3", number: 3, question: "RunSpecifiedTests vs RunLocalTests vs RunAllTests", answer: "RunSpecifiedTests: fastest, runs only named test classes, coverage counted only for classes covered by those tests, used in CI for targeted validation of a feature branch. Coverage requirement: 75% for each deployed Apex class. RunLocalTests: runs all non-managed-package tests, comprehensive, used for production deployments, coverage requirement is 75% org-wide. RunAllTests: runs everything including managed packages, slowest, can time out in large orgs with slow managed package tests. Use RunAllTests only when managed package tests are required for coverage and in non-time-sensitive full regression scenarios."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_2_4", number: 4, question: "When Salesforce requires RunLocalTests or higher for production deployments", answer: "Salesforce requires test execution (at minimum RunLocalTests) for any production deployment that includes Apex classes or triggers. Deployments that do NOT include Apex (metadata-only: layouts, fields, custom objects, reports) can use NoTestRun, which skips test execution and is much faster. For CI validation deployments (not production): you can use NoTestRun or RunSpecifiedTests to keep pipeline duration short. For production: if you use RunSpecifiedTests, you must specify tests that cover all deployed Apex components at 75%. If you skip tests using NoTestRun on a production Apex deployment, the deployment is rejected."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_2_5", number: 5, question: "Managing test execution time in large orgs", answer: "Strategies to keep CI pipeline duration acceptable: (1) RunSpecifiedTests in CI (not RunLocalTests); map test classes to the components they cover in a manifest. (2) Parallel test execution: Salesforce runs tests in parallel by default; avoid test anti-patterns that break parallelism (shared static state, SeeAllData=true). (3) Test isolation: ensure @testSetup is used correctly so data is created efficiently. (4) Identify and fix slow test classes (Developer Console, Tooling API coverage reports). (5) For production, run RunLocalTests in a separate job while feature validation jobs use RunSpecifiedTests. Target: CI validation under 20 minutes; full regression under 60 minutes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_2_6", number: 6, question: "Test coverage degradation risk in package-based development", answer: "In package-based development, each package has its own test classes. When a package is installed in an org, its tests contribute to org-wide coverage. Risk: if a dependent package is not tested (RunLocalTests excludes managed package tests from coverage calculation), and that package contains untested Apex, coverage may appear higher than it actually is. Ensure dependent package tests run and are counted: use RunAllTests when managed packages contribute significant Apex. In unlocked package development: each package must independently maintain 75% coverage for its own Apex components. Use 'sf apex run test' scoped to the package directory during CI to validate package-level coverage."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "test_obj_3",
                    title: "Describe and recommend a unified test data strategy",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_3_1", number: 1, question: "Unified test data strategy design", answer: "A unified test data strategy provides consistent, representative, and secure test data across all environments. Components: (1) Data inventory: catalog what data types are needed per environment tier (unit tests need minimal data; UAT needs production-representative data). (2) Data generation: use test data factories for unit tests; use masked production data for QA/UAT sandboxes. (3) Data governance: never use real PII in non-production environments; enforce data masking policies. (4) Data lifecycle: align data refresh cycles with sandbox refresh cycles. (5) Data seeding automation: scripts that reseed environments post-refresh. Goal: any developer or tester can onboard to any environment and have the data they need without manual setup."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_3_2", number: 2, question: "Partial Copy sandbox and test data seeding", answer: "A Partial Copy sandbox includes production metadata plus a sample of production data (configured via sandbox template - up to 10,000 records per selected object). This makes it the ideal source of representative data for QA testing without the cost and refresh limitations of a Full Copy sandbox. Use it for: QA test execution where realistic data relationships are needed. For seeding lower environments (Developer sandboxes, scratch orgs): export data from the Partial Copy sandbox using CLI data export commands, apply masking to remove PII, then import into lower environments using CLI data import. This creates a consistent, representative dataset that developers and QA can rely on."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_3_3", number: 3, question: "Data masking and anonymization strategies for Salesforce test environments", answer: "Data masking transforms production PII into realistic but fake data before it is placed in non-production environments. Techniques: (1) Substitution: replace real names/emails with fake ones (e.g., John Smith > Test User 1). (2) Shuffling: shuffle values within a column so field types remain realistic but are no longer associated with real people. (3) Pseudonymization: replace identifiable values with tokens, keeping a mapping table in a secure location. Tools: Salesforce Data Mask (native AppExchange tool), Informatica, or custom Apex batch scripts. Masking should happen BEFORE a Full Copy or Partial Copy sandbox is given to non-privileged users. Document which fields require masking in a data classification policy."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "test_3_4", number: 4, question: "Test data lifecycle management", answer: "Test data becomes stale when: sandboxes are refreshed (data is overwritten or cleared), the production data model changes (new required fields, changed relationships), business processes change (old test scenarios no longer reflect real flows). Prevention: (1) Store test data seeding scripts in source control alongside code - they are updated when the schema changes. (2) Automate post-sandbox-refresh data seeding as part of the sandbox refresh pipeline. (3) Review test data quarterly against current business processes. (4) Tag test data records with a creation timestamp and scenario name for easy identification and cleanup. Stale test data leads to false-passing tests and QA frustration."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Domain: Releasing (13%) — 16 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-releasing",
            title: "Releasing (13%)",
            color: Color(red: 0.50, green: 0.25, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.96, green: 0.92, blue: 0.96),
            icon: "arrow.right.circle.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "rel_obj_1",
                    title: "Analyze and explain use cases and considerations for managed, unmanaged, and unlocked packages",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_1", number: 1, question: "Managed package use cases", answer: "Managed packages are used by ISVs (Independent Software Vendors) to distribute Salesforce applications on AppExchange. Key characteristics: namespace required, IP protected (code is obfuscated/locked), subscriber upgrade lifecycle managed by the publisher (1GP or 2GP), installed via AppExchange or install URL. Limitations post-release: cannot remove fields, objects, or classes from a released managed package version (they become deprecated); cannot change API names of existing components. Use managed packages when: you are building a product for external customer distribution, IP protection is required, and you need a formal subscriber upgrade model."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_2", number: 2, question: "Unlocked package use cases", answer: "Unlocked packages (2GP) are used for internal modular deployment within a customer's own orgs. No namespace required (unless desired). Source-driven: components are managed in source control, packages are built from that source. Components are not IP-protected. Ideal for: decomposing a large org into manageable modules (Account domain, Billing domain, etc.), enabling independent release of different org areas, standardizing shared components across multiple internal orgs. Unlike managed packages, you can evolve the package more freely (with caveats around destructive changes in subscriber orgs)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_3", number: 3, question: "Choosing between managed, unmanaged, and unlocked packages", answer: "Managed: choose when building an AppExchange product for external customers, IP protection needed, formal upgrade lifecycle required. Unmanaged: choose for one-time template distribution (e.g., a starter kit), development samples, or when you want the installer to own and modify the code freely with no upgrade path. Unlocked: choose for internal delivery, modular org architecture, source-driven development, when upgrade-ability with some flexibility is needed. Key question: will subscribers upgrade this package over time? If yes, choose managed (ISV) or unlocked (internal). If it is a one-time install with no future upgrades, unmanaged may suffice."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_4", number: 4, question: "Package versioning: major vs. minor versions and semantics", answer: "Package versions follow semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.BUILD). MAJOR: significant release with potentially breaking changes (new dependencies, removed components). Subscribers must manually opt in. MINOR: new features added backward-compatibly; subscribers can upgrade with lower risk. PATCH: bug fixes; lowest risk upgrade. BUILD: internal build number (auto-incremented). In Salesforce 2GP: 'sf package version create' generates a new version; 'Released' flag marks a version as production-ready and immutable. Beta versions can be used for testing before releasing. Key: once a package version is released, it cannot be changed - create a new version for any changes."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_5", number: 5, question: "Package dependencies: defining and managing dependencies between unlocked packages", answer: "In a multi-package project, packages can depend on other packages. Dependencies are declared in sfdx-project.json under the 'dependencies' array for each package directory. Dependencies specify: package ID and version number. At install time, dependent packages must be installed first. Circular dependencies are not allowed - maintain a clean dependency hierarchy (foundational packages at the bottom, application packages at the top). Use a package dependency graph to visualize and manage complexity. CI pipeline should build and validate packages in dependency order. Changes to a foundational package require re-testing all packages that depend on it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_6", number: 6, question: "Namespace considerations for managed vs. unlocked packages", answer: "Managed packages: a namespace is REQUIRED. The namespace prefix is added to all component API names (e.g., MyNamespace__MyField__c). This prevents conflicts with subscriber org components but makes component names verbose and harder to change. Unlocked packages: a namespace is OPTIONAL. Without a namespace, component API names remain the same as in source control. With a namespace, same rules as managed packages apply. Implications: namespaced packages are harder to migrate to a different namespace later. Choose namespace for: multi-subscriber distribution (managed) or when you want component isolation across orgs. Namespace decisions are essentially permanent - plan carefully."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_1_7", number: 7, question: "Deprecated components and subscriber impact during package upgrades", answer: "In managed packages, you cannot delete a component from a released version - you must deprecate it. Deprecation marks the component as 'do not use' but it remains installed in subscriber orgs. Subscribers see deprecation warnings in setup. Impact: deprecated Apex classes still count toward governor limits; deprecated fields still occupy the subscriber's field limit. Subscribers should migrate away from deprecated components before the publisher removes support. For unlocked packages: you can remove components in newer versions, but removal will fail in subscriber orgs that have customizations or data dependencies on the removed component. Always communicate deprecation/removal with a migration guide."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "rel_obj_2",
                    title: "Apply sandbox strategy to a specific release plan",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_2_1", number: 1, question: "Mapping a sandbox environment strategy to a release plan with multiple parallel streams", answer: "For three parallel streams (e.g., Feature A, Feature B, Hotfix): each stream gets its own Developer or Developer Pro sandbox for isolated development. All streams merge into a shared Integration sandbox for combined testing. Integration builds deploy to a QA sandbox (Partial Copy) for QA sign-off. After QA, changes promote to UAT sandbox (Partial Copy) for business validation. After UAT, all approved streams are bundled and deployed to Staging (Full Copy) for pre-production validation. Finally, production deployment. The key is that streams are isolated in development but converge progressively as they move up the pipeline."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_2_2", number: 2, question: "Staging sandbox role in the release plan", answer: "The Staging sandbox (Full Copy) serves as the final pre-production gate. Its role: (1) Validate the complete deployment package in a production-equivalent environment (same metadata, same data volume, same integrations). (2) Execute performance and load testing to identify governor limit issues at scale. (3) Provide a final UAT opportunity for stakeholders who need to sign off on production-equivalent behavior. (4) Validate rollback procedures in a safe environment before production. Staging must be refreshed from production after every production release to maintain parity. It should never be used for development - treat it as read-only except for authorized release deployments."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_2_3", number: 3, question: "Hotfix stream integration into an ongoing release plan", answer: "When a critical production bug occurs mid-release cycle: (1) Branch the hotfix from the production Git tag (not from the in-progress release branch). (2) Fix and test in the dedicated hotfix sandbox. (3) Fast-track through Staging if time permits (or skip to production with documented risk acceptance if truly critical). (4) Deploy to production. (5) Immediately merge the hotfix changes back to the main/develop branch and all in-flight feature branches. (6) Redeploy to Integration/QA/UAT sandboxes to ensure the hotfix is included in the next regular release. Conflict resolution: if the hotfix conflicts with in-flight feature work, resolve the conflict in the feature branch, not in the hotfix branch."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_2_4", number: 4, question: "Integrating a training environment into the release plan", answer: "The training sandbox (typically Developer Pro or Partial Copy) is used for end-user training on new features before they go to production. When to refresh: refresh it after a UAT sign-off so that training users can practice on the exact features about to go live, but not so close to the production deployment that training is disrupted by last-minute changes. Isolation: training sandbox must NEVER receive in-progress development changes - it should only receive officially released (UAT-approved) feature sets. Data ownership: the training team owns the data in the training sandbox; use representative but fake records. The Release Manager should coordinate the training sandbox refresh as part of the release calendar."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "rel_obj_3",
                    title: "Describe and recommend an appropriate release management strategy",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_3_1", number: 1, question: "Release management process: change freeze, go/no-go, release notes, post-release monitoring", answer: "Change freeze: a period (typically 1-2 weeks before a major release) during which no new features can be introduced to the release pipeline; only bug fixes and critical changes allowed. Go/no-go criteria: defined checklist that must be fully satisfied before production deployment is authorized (e.g., all UAT test cases passed, no open P1 defects, CAB approved, rollback plan documented). Release notes: a document for stakeholders describing what is changing, any user-facing behavior changes, and what was fixed. Post-release monitoring: monitor key metrics for 24-48 hours post-deployment (error logs, support ticket volume, key business process completion rates, Apex exception emails)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_3_2", number: 2, question: "Release cadence selection", answer: "Continuous delivery (CD): deploy to production whenever a feature is ready (after automated gates pass); requires high test automation maturity and feature flag capability. Best for product teams with low change risk and high confidence in automation. Sprint-based: deploy at the end of each sprint (every 2 weeks); predictable, manageable, good for teams transitioning from waterfall. Most common in Salesforce implementations. Scheduled major release: quarterly or bi-annual large bundled releases; lowest risk appetite, highest governance overhead; common in regulated industries. Match cadence to business risk tolerance, support team capacity, and change management capability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_3_3", number: 3, question: "Coordinating a release across multiple Salesforce orgs", answer: "Multi-org releases require: (1) Shared release calendar with deployment windows for each org. (2) Dependency sequencing - if Org A provides an API consumed by Org B, deploy Org A first. (3) Shared reference data synchronization - if both orgs share reference data (e.g., product catalog), synchronize this data before deploying the code that uses it. (4) Coordinated rollback - define whether rolling back one org requires rolling back all orgs (usually yes, to maintain integration compatibility). (5) Cross-org regression testing - validate integrations between orgs post-deployment. A Release Manager or Release Train Engineer must own cross-org coordination."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_3_4", number: 4, question: "Feature flagging and dark launching in Salesforce", answer: "Feature flagging: deploy code to production but keep it inactive until a flag is switched on. In Salesforce: use Custom Metadata Type records (e.g., Feature_Flag__mdt with an IsActive__c checkbox) queried by Apex at runtime. Use Permission Sets to control which users see new UI elements (Lightning components check for permissions). Dark launching: code is deployed and running (for load testing, logging, side effects) but its results are not exposed to users. Benefits: decouple deployment from release (deploy on Tuesday, activate feature on Friday without redeployment), instant rollback by flipping a flag, gradual rollout to specific user groups. Avoid using Custom Settings for feature flags - prefer CMT for deployability."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "rel_3_5", number: 5, question: "Blue-green and canary release patterns applied to Salesforce", answer: "Blue-green: maintain two identical production environments (blue = current live, green = new version deployed and validated). Switch traffic from blue to green when ready; rollback is instant by switching back. In Salesforce, true blue-green is not natively supported (there is only one production org). Approximate it with: feature flags + permission set-controlled activation (green is deployed but only accessible to a test group; blue behavior remains for all others). Canary release: gradually roll out a feature to a small percentage of users, monitor, then expand. Implement in Salesforce with: permission set assignments (assign the 'new feature' permission set to 5% of users first), custom metadata flags with user/profile conditions, or Experience Cloud audience targeting."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Domain: Operating (10%) — 11 topics
        StudySection(
            id: "pdlda-operating",
            title: "Operating (10%)",
            color: Color(red: 0.35, green: 0.45, blue: 0.30),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.93, green: 0.95, blue: 0.92),
            icon: "gearshape.2.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "ops_obj_1",
                    title: "Explain the implications of incorporating requests directly in a production environment",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_1_1", number: 1, question: "Implications of making configuration changes directly in production", answer: "Direct production changes: (1) Break the source of truth - source control no longer reflects what is in production. (2) Cause configuration drift - future deployments from source control may overwrite or conflict with the direct change. (3) Bypass quality gates - no peer review, no CI validation, no automated tests. (4) Create audit gaps - depending on tool, the change may not be traceable to a business requirement or approver. (5) Risk downstream sandboxes becoming out-of-sync - lower environments do not know about the change until it is back-propagated. In regulated environments, direct production changes may violate SOX, HIPAA, or other compliance controls."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_1_2", number: 2, question: "Urgent production change request under pressure", answer: "When stakeholders demand an immediate production change, evaluate: (1) What is the actual risk of NOT making the change right now? (quantify business impact: revenue at risk, users blocked, compliance violation). (2) What is the risk of making the change directly? (configuration drift, missed dependencies, no rollback path). (3) Is the change truly so urgent it cannot go through even a fast-track process (1-2 hours)? (4) If proceeding directly: document the reason, get verbal/email approval from a designated authority, make the minimal change needed, and commit to retrieving and back-propagating the change within 24 hours. Never let urgency become the default path to production."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_1_3", number: 3, question: "Emergency change process design", answer: "A sanctioned emergency change process allows fast production changes while preserving governance. Key elements: (1) Pre-defined trigger criteria (what qualifies as an emergency). (2) Pre-authorized approvers (a list of individuals who can authorize emergency changes without a full CAB meeting). (3) Streamlined process: developer makes change in hotfix sandbox > gets approval from pre-authorized approver > deploys to production > documents the change in the change management system within 4 hours. (4) Mandatory post-change steps: retrieve from production, commit to source control, back-propagate to lower environments, complete formal change record. (5) Post-incident review: analyze root cause and how to prevent recurrence."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "ops_obj_2",
                    title: "Explain implications of direct production changes and steps to integrate into ALM",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_2_1", number: 1, question: "Steps to integrate a direct production change back into the ALM pipeline", answer: "Process: (1) Retrieve the changed metadata from production using 'sf project retrieve start' targeting the specific components that were changed. (2) Create a branch in Git (e.g., 'hotfix/direct-prod-change-YYYYMMDD'). (3) Commit the retrieved metadata to the branch with a message explaining what was changed, why, and who authorized it. (4) Create a PR for peer review - even though the change is already in production, code review documents the change for future reference. (5) Merge to main branch after review. (6) Deploy from source control to all lower sandboxes (Integration, QA, UAT, Staging) so they are in sync with production. (7) Update the change management ticket."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_2_2", number: 2, question: "Detecting and reconciling configuration drift", answer: "Drift detection approaches: (1) Tools: Gearset, Copado Org Differences, Salesforce DevOps Center change tracking, or custom scripts comparing Metadata API retrieval outputs from production vs. source control. (2) Manual: periodic 'org compare' runs that diff production against the main branch in source control. (3) Proactive: enable source-tracking sandboxes or scratch orgs to detect uncommitted changes in development. Reconciliation process: identify the delta (what is in production but not in source control, and vice versa), evaluate each delta item (intentional change or drift?), retrieve intentional changes and commit them, deploy missing source-controlled changes to production through the normal pipeline."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_2_3", number: 3, question: "Governance controls to prevent unauthorized direct production changes", answer: "Controls: (1) Production org lockdown: restrict 'Modify All Data' and 'Customize Application' permissions to a service account used only by the CI/CD pipeline. Remove these permissions from admin profiles. (2) Change approval workflows: require all changes to be submitted via a change management system (ServiceNow, Jira) before any deployment is authorized. (3) Audit trail monitoring: configure Salesforce Event Monitoring to alert on direct logins to production and configuration changes not made by the CI/CD service account. (4) Segregation of duties: the person who develops a change cannot be the person who deploys it to production. (5) Regular access reviews: quarterly review of who has production admin access."),
                    ]
                ),
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "ops_obj_3",
                    title: "Compare and contrast approaches for managing common release artifacts in a multi-org scenario",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_3_1", number: 1, question: "Strategies for managing shared metadata components across multiple Salesforce orgs", answer: "Three approaches: (1) Packages: shared components are built into an unlocked (or managed) package, versioned, and installed in all target orgs. Gold standard - changes are versioned and tracked. (2) Template repos: a shared Git repository contains the canonical version of shared metadata; each org's pipeline pulls from this repo. Less automated but more flexible. (3) Manual synchronization: admins manually replicate changes across orgs. Highest risk of divergence, not scalable. Packages are the recommended approach for mature orgs. Template repos work for teams not yet ready for full package-based development. Manual sync should be eliminated as soon as possible."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_3_2", number: 2, question: "Using unlocked packages to share and version common components across multiple orgs", answer: "In the package subscriber model: a 'common' or 'foundation' unlocked package contains shared components (utility Apex classes, common custom objects, shared layouts). Each consuming org installs this package as a dependency. When a shared component is updated: create a new package version, test it in a scratch org, release it, then update the dependency version in each consuming org's sfdx-project.json and trigger that org's CI pipeline to install the updated version. Update propagation: consuming orgs must explicitly upgrade to the new version (unlike automatic managed package updates, unlocked package subscribers control their own upgrade timing)."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_3_3", number: 3, question: "Release artifact governance in a multi-org landscape", answer: "Governance requirements: (1) Ownership: define which team/org owns each shared component and is responsible for its quality and backward compatibility. (2) Approval: changes to shared components require higher-level approval (cross-org impact means more stakeholders). (3) Promotion: shared component changes must be tested in ALL consuming orgs before being released, not just the originating org. (4) Versioning: strict semantic versioning for shared packages to prevent breaking changes from silently propagating. (5) Communication: a changelog or release notes for each shared package update, distributed to all consuming org teams. Without clear governance, shared components become a source of cross-org breakage."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_3_4", number: 4, question: "Cross-org change management coordination", answer: "When changes affect multiple connected orgs: (1) Dependency tracking: map which orgs consume which shared components; maintain a dependency registry. (2) Aligned release windows: schedule deployment windows across all affected orgs in the correct dependency order (foundational orgs first, consuming orgs second). (3) Regression testing: after deploying to a consuming org, run integration tests that exercise the cross-org interface (APIs, events, data shares). (4) Cross-org rollback plan: if a shared component update breaks a consuming org, the rollback must be coordinated - rolling back the shared package in one org while others have upgraded can cause API version mismatches. (5) Communication: a shared release calendar visible to all org teams."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "ops_3_5", number: 5, question: "Event logs and deployment history for post-release monitoring and diagnosis", answer: "Post-release monitoring tools: (1) Salesforce Event Monitoring (add-on): provides detailed event logs (API calls, logins, Apex exceptions, report exports) queryable via EventLogFile object. Use to detect unexpected behavior or errors after a deployment. (2) Apex Exception Email notifications: configure in Setup to email developers when unhandled Apex exceptions occur in production - the first alert of a deployment issue. (3) Deployment history: in Setup > Deployment Status, view past deployments and their results. (4) Debug logs: targeted logging for specific users immediately after deployment to diagnose issues. (5) Health Check and Salesforce Optimizer: periodic org health assessments. Establish a post-release monitoring checklist with specific log queries to run 24 hours after every production deployment."),
                    ]
                )
            ]
        )

    ]
}
